1) Aspirin's long story began oound 4000 years ago when Egyptians and Greeks were using leaves from willow trees to reduce pain and fever.
2) In 1763, The Royal Society published an article written by Edward Stone which recommends the use of powdered willow bark as a treatment against malaria.
3) The active ingredient of willow - salicin was not isolated until 1829 when the French pharmacist Pierre-Joseph Leroux extracted crystals of slicin.
4) Salicin is effective in treating inflammations but causes stomach irritation.
5) The German chemist Felix Hoffmann had a father who suffered from inflammatory rheumatism and was in search for a product without side effects.
6) On the 10th October or 1897, Hoffmann, who worked for Bayer Pharmaceuticals, publshed apaper explaining the method to obtain acetylsalicylic acid: the modern aspirin.
7) The French and German chemistshad already produced acetylsalicylic acid in 1853 and 1855 and did not realise the importance of their discovery.
8) in 1900, Bayer obtained the patent for aspirin and made a fortune by selling this new product worldwide.
9) Aspirin has been in public domain since Bayer's patent expired in 1919.
10) Today, hundreds of companies manufacture this product
11) Every year, 11,600 tons of aspirin are sold, which is about 30 aspirin per person per year.
12) Now, aspirin is over 100 years old and doctors just begin to understand how it works and still find new uses.
